{"id":39,"course_id":13,"title":"Secrets, Environments, and Rollback Reality","summary":"Promote changes through Git while keeping secrets and recovery honest.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Promotion and secrets\nGitOps makes release intent visible, but it does not make plaintext secrets acceptable or rollback automatic. A production promotion should show what changed, which environment values changed, and how recovery works.\n- Do not commit plaintext secrets.\n- Use External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, SOPS, or another documented pattern.\n- Promote the same image digest or chart version when possible.\n- Rollback may mean reverting Git, syncing a prior chart version, or disabling a feature flag.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ git diff main..release/prod -- environments/prod\n$ argocd app history checkout-prod\n$ kubectl get externalsecret,sealedsecret -A\n$ argocd app rollback checkout-prod REVISION\nTreat rollback commands as syntax practice unless you operate an approved local ArgoCD instance.\n\n## Failure mode\nIf auto-sync remains enabled during manual rollback, ArgoCD may reapply the bad desired state.","body_traditional":"## Promotion and secrets\nGitOps makes release intent visible, but it does not make plaintext secrets acceptable or rollback automatic. A production promotion should show what changed, which environment values changed, and how recovery works.\n- Do not commit plaintext secrets.\n- Use External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, SOPS, or another documented pattern.\n- Promote the same image digest or chart version when possible.\n- Rollback may mean reverting Git, syncing a prior chart version, or disabling a feature flag.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ git diff main..release/prod -- environments/prod\n$ argocd app history checkout-prod\n$ kubectl get externalsecret,sealedsecret -A\n$ argocd app rollback checkout-prod REVISION\nTreat rollback commands as syntax practice unless you operate an approved local ArgoCD instance.\n\n## Failure mode\nIf auto-sync remains enabled during manual rollback, ArgoCD may reapply the bad desired state.","pinyin":"Scenario: A production sync introduced bad config.\nChecklist:\n- Identify whether the change was image, config, chart, CRD, or data.\n- Pause auto-sync if needed.\n- Prefer reverting the Git change so desired state matches recovery.\n- Note rollback limits for database or external state.\nCommands:\n$ argocd app history checkout-prod\n$ git log --oneline -- environments/prod/checkout","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-argocd-gitops","course_category":"ArgoCD","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":166,"simplified":"ExternalSecret","traditional":"ExternalSecret","pinyin":"Secret sync","definition":"A resource that syncs values from an external secret provider."},{"id":167,"simplified":"SealedSecret","traditional":"SealedSecret","pinyin":"Encrypted secret","definition":"An encrypted secret manifest decryptable by a cluster controller."},{"id":168,"simplified":"SOPS","traditional":"SOPS","pinyin":"Secret encryption","definition":"A tool for encrypting selected fields in files stored in Git."},{"id":169,"simplified":"Promotion","traditional":"Promotion","pinyin":"Release flow","definition":"Moving reviewed artifact and configuration through environments."},{"id":170,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Recovery","definition":"Returning service behavior to a known-good state while respecting external state."}],"flashcards":[{"id":100,"lesson_id":39,"prompt":"Name three GitOps secret patterns.","answer":"External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, and SOPS-encrypted files.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":101,"lesson_id":39,"prompt":"Why is reverting Git often the clean rollback?","answer":"It keeps Git as desired state so ArgoCD does not fight recovery.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":102,"lesson_id":39,"prompt":"What can make rollback unsafe?","answer":"Database migrations, external state, CRDs, and generated credentials.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
